A||||8||ChadMan||Wax on, wax off||||5||
Z||000000||ChadMan||06-20-2000||09:21 AM||||It finally quit raining long enough for me to wash my Coupe last night, and I need to wax it before the Mid-Ohio show this weekend.  The car is covered with very shallow longitudinal scratches, left over from when I had no choice but to scrub it at the coin-op car wash after it got egged last winter.<P>I know some of you guys are detailing wizards.  What kind of wax would you recommend to remove these scratches and leave a nice shine?  (TIA)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||06-20-2000||10:01 AM||||To remove the fine "car wash" scratches, you can use Meguires Body Scrub. This will remove fine scratches and blemishes. Next use Meguires Deep Crystal Polish, This will buff all the paint to a glass like finish. Finally, wax with Meguires Deep Crystal Carnuba wax. (do I sound like a Meguires commercial yet?)  I have about 90% factory original paint on my car and it shines like new! (all hand buffed with Meguires)<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image04_1.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||06-20-2000||11:49 AM||||I'll second Oreif's recommendations.  Meguires makes a lot of good stuff for cleaning the Fiero!  If it's really beat it may take 4 hours or so (benefits of a small car).  A random orbital polisher is great for putting stuff on. Lots of cotton rags to take the stuff off.  Lots of cotten rags!  Change frequently. All the auto parts places have the cotten rags. $4/dozen.  Wash and dry afterword, they'll look cruddy, but work fine.||||reg||14
Z||000003||ChadMan||06-20-2000||01:51 PM||||Thanks, guys.  I will be happy if it looks half as good as Oreif's when I get it done.<P>It's clouding up out there right now - perfect car-buffing weather unless it starts raining.  Wonder if the boss will notice if I duck out a little early...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||frontal lobe||06-20-2000||02:39 PM||||Oreif,<P>I could almost make out my reflection on your car RIGHT ON THE COMPUTER MONITOR!!  Wow, nice work!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fret Naught||06-20-2000||03:12 PM||||Here is a question, I got my car back from the body shop last weekend, had the hood re done, I asked the guy when I could wax it and he said 90 days min. He said he doesn't use wax, that if I wanted I could use glaze anytime, and that would give me that "wet" look, he also said meguires puts out a glaze product. Orief is that the deep crystal polish you are talking about? you definitly have the look and shine I want.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fldevel||06-20-2000||06:22 PM||||i use meguiars also great stuff ..hey oreif ..if u had a red pin stripe our cars would almost be identical <p>[This message has been edited by fldevel (edited 06-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||06-20-2000||06:40 PM||||The Deep Crystal Polish is not a glaze. A glaze is a type of coating that keeps a gloss film over the paint to give it the "wet" look. The Deep Crystal is a polish for the paint. It also adds silicone into the paint to aid in preventing chips and cracks. After you use the polish you can glaze it or wax it. Glazing works very well on laqure (sp)paints and OK on enamel. I prefer waxing because it's a slightly thicker coating and protects the paint better. Glazing is very glossy but thin. It's a matter of preference But I do know that waxing a freshly painted car is not a good idea until the paint has "aged" I always thought the time frame was 30 days but it might depend on the brand/type of paint used.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ChadMan||06-20-2000||07:06 PM||||Well, I just finished waxing my car - clouds were rolling in, so I didn't have time to do the good 3-step Meguiar's procedure Oreif recommended.  I used plain ole Tortoise Wax instead.<P>But I'm happy - it looks much, much better than it did and the scratches are much less noticeable!<P>This wax job is good enough for the Mid-Ohio show, but I will try the Oreif/Old Lar method the week before the FOCOA meet!||||reg||6
